        "thinking": "The agent returned a very large output. Let me read it from the file it was saved to, but given the size and complexity, let me approach this differently.\n\nThe key insight here is that implementing all these raw Express handlers as Hono handlers manually is going to be:\n1. Very tedious and error-prone\n2. Hard to keep in sync with the server code\n\nA better approach is to build a **generic bridge** that can run raw Express-style handlers in the CF context. Let me design this bridge:\n\nThe bridge needs to:\n1. Create a mock Express `req` object with:\n   - `params`: from URL matching\n   - `query`: from URL search params\n   - `body`: parsed from request body\n   - `actor`: from CF actor resolution\n   - `method`, `path`, `headers`\n2. Create a mock Express `res` object that captures:\n   - `res.json(data)` → creates a JSON Response\n   - `res.status(n)` → chainable, sets status\n   - `res.send(data)` → creates a Response\n   - `res.set(header, value)` → sets response header\n   - `res.end()` → ends response\n\nThen I can register routes by wrapping the existing Express handlers in a Hono-compatible adapter that translates between the two frameworks. I'm realizing the handlers in access.ts aren't exported separately—they're baked into the router registration itself. So instead of trying to extract individual handlers, I should build a more comprehensive bridge that walks the entire router stack and converts both tagged and untagged routes into Hono handlers, creating mock req/res objects to call the raw Express handlers underneath.\n\nThe tricky part is mocking the request and response objects correctly. The access.ts handlers expect specific properties like route params, query strings, the parsed body from middleware, and the actor context that gets attached during CF resolution. For responses, they use `res.json()` and `res.status().json()` patterns.\n\nI also need to account for the access service methods that do database reads and writes, plus the permission assertion functions that check `req.actor` and throw `HttpError` when unauthorized.\n\nBuilding a bridge to connect these pieces...\n\nDefining the request and response interfaces... Writing the response handler... Finishing the response object setup...\n\nNow I'm realizing the handlers in access.ts are closures that capture the services created inside the factory function — `accessService`, `boardAuthService`, `agents`, and others. So when I call `accessRoutes(db, opts)` in the CF environment, it returns a real router with those closures intact. I can walk through the router's stack, extract the raw handlers, and invoke them with mock request/response objects to bridge them to Hono.\n\nThe key challenge is handling the middleware chain properly — the validate middleware calls `next()` to pass control to the main handler, so my bridge needs to simulate that flow by executing each handler in the stack sequentially and capturing the response. I need to handle error propagation through the middleware chain by converting Express errors into HTTP responses in the CF context. The tricky part is that some routes use raw Express handlers while others are wrapped with `expressHandler`, so I need to distinguish between them when deciding whether to use the Express bridge or CF handling path.\n\nFor the untagged routes in `access.ts` and `company-skills.ts`, I'm planning to create a bridge function that walks the router stack and registers each untagged route as a Hono handler that uses the Express bridge.\n\nWriting the bridge function... Writing request/response handling... I need to handle the body parsing differently in Cloudflare — I'll parse the JSON before setting up the request object, since the validate middleware expects `req.body` to already be available. I'm also looking at how `assertCompanyAccess` and `isLocalImplicit` check the actor object to understand what properties need to be set.\n\nSince `resolveActorFromRequest` already handles actor resolution, I can reuse that same logic rather than reimplementing it. Now I'm sketching out the bridge implementation with helper functions to create mock request and response objects, execute the middleware chain, and wrap the Express router for Cloudflare.\n\nThe tricky part is parameter extraction — Hono extracts route params via `c.req.param()`, but the Express handlers expect them in `req.params`. Since Express uses the same `:paramName` syntax as Hono, I can map them directly by parsing the route pattern and pulling values from Hono's context.\n\nNow I'm setting up the bridge implementation with mock request and response objects that translate between Hono's context and what Express handlers expect.\n\nActually, I need to reconsider this approach — `access.ts` isn't currently part of the Cloudflare bundle, and importing it would pull in Node.js-specific dependencies like the crypto module that won't work in a Worker environment. The issue is that `access.ts` has a top-level import of `node:dns/promises`, which isn't available in CF Workers. Even though esbuild will mark it as external, the import statement itself will fail at runtime since CF doesn't have a polyfill for it. I need to check the build configuration to see if `node:dns/promises` is actually listed in the external modules array, because if it's not handled properly, bundling this file will break the worker. `node:dns` is NOT in the EXTERNAL list! This means esbuild will try to bundle it, and it'll fail.\n\nSo I can't directly import `access.ts` in the CF build without also adding shims for `node:dns/promises`.\n\nAlternatively, I could add `\"node:dns/promises\"` to the EXTERNAL list and create a shim that throws an error if called. Since the handlers that use DNS are not called in the code paths I care about, this would work.\n\nOr, I could take a different approach: import only specific helper functions from within `access.ts` that don't transitively import the problematic modules.\n\nBut `access.ts` is a single large file, and even if I only use specific functions, the entire file gets imported with all its top-level imports.\n\nI'm weighing three main options: adding a DNS shim, refactoring `access.ts` to isolate the problematic imports, or implementing the missing routes inline in CF without importing `access.ts` at all. I've already done the inline approach for `loadCompanyUserDirectory` and `loadJoinRequestRecords`, so I could extend that pattern to other routes. The shim approach would be quicker—just marking DNS as external and throwing an error if it's called—but I'd still need to figure out how to bridge to the raw handlers. I'm leaning toward a hybrid approach where I add a DNS shim to unblock the import.",
